  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 PM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nHOT WITH SCATTERED SHOWERS AND STORMS TODAY:\n\nHigh pressure remains centered south of the Four Corners with \nhot and predominately dry conditions continuing for one more \nday. Some remnant moisture remains trapped under the high which \nwill result in scattered afternoon storms over the high terrain.\nFlash flooding cannot be ruled out under stronger storms, but \nthe threat is very low and primarily contained to recent burn \nscars and susceptible terrain.\n\nThe pressure gradient will tighten a bit today as a low \npressure system moves into the Pacific Northwest, allowing for \nbreezy afternoon winds especially across northeast Utah and \nnorthwest Colorado. Gusty and erratic winds are also anticipated\nnear thunderstorms this afternoon with a dry near-surface layer\nin place. As a result, an isolated severe storm or two cannot \nbe ruled out with damaging wind being the primary concern. \n\nCOOLER AND WETTER THIS WEEKEND AND BEYOND: \n\nA substantial pattern change arrives tonight as high pressure \nbegins to break down with a broad trough approaching from the \nwest. Nocturnal convection is likely as moisture advection \nintensifies within the southwesterly flow aloft. This will set \nmost of eastern Utah and western Colorado up for a cloudy \nSaturday with chances for multiple rounds of showers and storms \nas PWATs surge to ~175% of normal. Most notably, a strong upper-\nlevel jet will provide enhanced shear along and north of I-70. \nThis will support the potential for a few strong to severe \nstorms with damaging wind gusts being the primary treat. \nTraining thunderstorms will also be capable of producing flash \nflooding.\n\nStrong storms develop again on Sunday, but with the jet \ndeparting to the northeast, flash flooding becomes the primary \nthreat of strong and/or training thunderstorms.\n\nEnsembles support PWATs of >150% of normal through Monday, \nbefore a slight downturn in moisture is likely for Tuesday and \npotentially Wednesday. Although an increase in model spread \nbegins to lower forecast confidence by mid week, another solid \npush of monsoonal moisture moves into the Southwest later in the\nweek. An eastern pacific tropical system is also possible next \nweek, so we'll be watching the track of that system to see if we\ncan potentially tap into that rich, tropical moisture later in \nthe week. Stay tuned!",
